{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "13",
    "case_summary": "A 22-year-old woman presented for evaluation of malaligned teeth. On examination, all permanent teeth had erupted except the third molars in both arches and the lower left lateral incisor. There was mild spacing between the lower left central incisor and the canine. She had Class II, Division 1 malocclusion with crowding in the maxillary anterior region. There was no history of prior dental extractions.\n\nA panoramic radiograph and a mandibular occlusal film showed an unerupted lower left lateral incisor with an associated well-defined radiopaque lesion in the left mandibular anterior region, surrounded by a thin radiolucent halo, which appeared to obstruct the eruption path of the tooth.",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Dental Malocclusion Syndrome with Unerupted Tooth and Radiopaque Lesion",
      "time_course": "subacute",
